# PART 5 — COMPLETE E2EE CRYPTOGRAPHIC ARCHITECTURE

This is a critical requirement.

Bitebend messaging must use genuine end-to-end encryption.

The backend, PostgreSQL, Railway Storage Bucket and Super Admin must not be able to decrypt ordinary customer message contents or media.

## 5.1 Protocol Evaluation

Evaluate maintained, production-suitable Signal-compatible protocol implementations.

Investigate:

- X3DH or PQXDH session establishment.
- Double Ratchet.
- Identity keys.
- Signed prekeys.
- One-time prekeys.
- Forward secrecy.
- Post-compromise security.
- Device identity verification.
- Key changes and security notifications.
- Browser/PWA compatibility.
- IndexedDB key persistence.
- WebCrypto compatibility.
- Multi-device support.
- Library maintenance, security review, licensing and platform support.

Do not invent a custom cryptographic protocol.

Do not assume WebCrypto alone is a Signal protocol implementation.

Do not select a library solely because it appears in the earlier audit. Verify current suitability.

If no suitable maintained library exists for the required browser architecture, explain the limitation and provide safe alternatives.

## 5.2 Customer and Restaurant Multi-Device Encryption

Restaurant messaging is not a simple one-to-one consumer chat.

A restaurant may have:

- Owner device.
- Multiple authorised waiter devices.
- Customer device.

Design per-device key management and message encryption.

Evaluate:

- Per-recipient-device encrypted message envelopes.
- Group messaging approaches.
- Sender-key suitability.
- Device addition.
- Device removal.
- Waiter reassignment.
- Staff deactivation.
- Lost device revocation.
- Conversation membership changes.
- Key rotation and rekeying.
- Historical message access after a participant is removed.

A removed waiter must not automatically retain future message access.

Do not assume deleting a staff account automatically revokes cryptographic access to all previously downloaded content.

Explain what historical access can and cannot be revoked.

## 5.3 Super Admin Privacy Boundary

Super Admin may access authorised customer phone PII.

However, this does not grant permission to decrypt E2EE messaging.

Clearly separate:

- Customer PII administration.
- Account administration.
- Conversation routing metadata.
- Encrypted message ciphertext.
- Encrypted multimedia.
- Device public keys.
- Private encryption keys.

The server must never hold a universal decryption key.

Investigate protection against server-side key substitution and malicious device registration.

Explain key verification/transparency requirements.

## 5.4 E2EE Metadata

